<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alcazaba of Málaga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Alcazaba of Málaga is a Moorish fortress-palace on a hill above the port of Málaga in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, built mainly in the 11th century and enlarged under the Nasrids. It stands above remains of the Phoenician and Roman town, beside a Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, and is linked by walls to the higher castle of Gibralfaro. It is one of the best-preserved Muslim fortresses in Spain.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alcazaba-of-malaga.jpg" alt="Alcazaba of Málaga" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fernando · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alcazaba_de_M%C3%A1laga_overview.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> occupied the hill from around 600 BCE, and remains of their fortification wall have been found there. In the Roman period it held a villa and industrial buildings, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> was built into its western slope in the 1st century. After the Muslim conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, sources mention a fortress with a mosque on the hill, built by Abd ar-Rahman I. The present Alcazaba was begun by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammudid-dynasty">Hammudid dynasty</a> in the early 11th century, probably under Yahya I, who ruled from 1021 to 1036. After the Zirids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> took Málaga in 1056, Badis added to it, perhaps the double walls.</p><p>In the early 14th century the Nasrid emir Muhammad II largely rebuilt the fortifications and the palaces, and Yusuf I built the Castle of Gibralfaro and the walled corridor joining it to the citadel. Ferdinand and Isabella took the city after the Siege of Málaga, which ended with its surrender on 18 August 1487, and raised their standard on the Torre del Homenaje. From the 18th century the fortress lost its military role and became a poor quarter of the city, until from 1933 Leopoldo Torres Balbás moved the residents out and began restoration and excavation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Alcazaba has two walled enclosures, one inside the other, each with towers, and visitors had to pass five gates to reach the palaces on the summit. Torres Balbás saw it as the prototype of military architecture in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> period. The way up is a bent entrance through the Puerta de la Bóveda, the Puerta de las Columnas, built with two Roman columns from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, and the Arco del Cristo, once a chapel. A well, the Pozo Airón, cuts 30 metres into the rock.</p><p>The inner enclosure is entered through the Puerta de los Cuartos de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, rebuilt in the 20th century. The southernmost palace, the only one of the 11th century, keeps an arcade of three horseshoe arches in a smaller form of the style of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madinat-al-zahra">Madinat al-Zahra</a>, and an annex with intersecting multifoil arches. North of it lies a Nasrid palace around the Patio de los <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naranjo">Naranjos</a> and the Patio de la Alberca, which keep their original pools and tile paving, and beyond it the Barrio Castrense, a quarter of eight houses for officials and servants, one of them with its own hammam.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alcazaba of Málaga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Phoenicians settled the bay of Málaga in the 8th century BCE, first at Cerro del Villar on an island in the mouth of the Guadalhorce, and then on the hill below the later <b>Alcazaba</b>, where Malaka grew into the main settlement by the 6th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/malaka">Malaka</a></li></ul></section>
